<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guglielmo Embriaco</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 1099–1102</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as William Embriaco, Testa di Maglio</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guglielmo Embriaco was a Genoese merchant and commander in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a>. In 1099 he dismantled his ships to build <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-engine">siege engines</a> for the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, and he later helped take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arsuf">Arsuf</a> and Caesarea, bringing rich booty back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>. His family, the Embriaci, became lords of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Gibelet</a> (Byblos) in the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guglielmo-embriaco.jpg" alt="Guglielmo Embriaco" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lodovico Pogliaghi · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Genova-Palazzo_San_Giorgio-DSCF7708.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
